title: User commands
tags: [[Customising the GUI]] Commands
kind: guide
aliases: commands.js user-commands.js user-commands custom-commands mf.invoke user:
summary: user-commands.js defines commands of your own in JavaScript — a name, the questions it asks, and what it runs, usually other commands through mf.invoke.

`~/.config/metafolder/gui/user-commands.js` is a JavaScript module. What it exports
is a set of commands: each key is a command's name, each value its definition. There
is nothing to register.

```
export default {
  'user:rate': {
    label: 'Rate the selected metarecords',
    args: [{ name: 'rating', prompt: () => 'Rating? (1-5)', complete: () => ['1', '2', '3', '4', '5'] }],
    run: (mf, rating) => mf.invoke(['metarecord:bulk', 'selection', 'set', 'rating', 'int', rating]),
  },
};
```

Such a command is a command like any other ([[Commands]]): it is listed in the
command input, asks for the arguments it was not given, can be bound to a key in
`keybindings.toml` ([[Changing keybindings]]) and run by a script.

!! A definition

|!Key |!Meaning |
|`run` |required: the function the command runs. It receives `mf`, then the arguments |
|`label` |what the command input shows beside the name (default: the name) |
|`args` |the arguments, in order ([[Interactive command arguments]]) |
|`log` |`false` keeps the command out of the message log (default: `true`) |

An argument has a `name` and, all optional: `prompt` (the question), `complete` (the
candidates: a function, or an array of views — [[Completion views]]), `initial`
(text already in the input), `optional`, `when` (whether to ask, given the earlier
answers) and `views`. Each of these functions receives `mf` first, where a panel's
receives nothing.

!! `mf`

`mf` is the object panels get as `metafolder`, with `mf.invoke([...])` to run a
command as if it were typed (below). A user command belongs to no panel, so:

* it acts in its ''execution context'' ([[Commands]]): the focused workspace and slot when it runs — `mf.workspace.get('active_repo')` is the repository of where you are now — and `mf.context` says which (`{ws, slot}`);
* `mf.withContext({ws, slot}, async (mf) => …)` runs a block elsewhere: inside it, `mf` and the commands it invokes act on that workspace and slot; outside, nothing has moved;
* `mf.atTarget(where, async (mf) => …, {targets})` runs a block at an [[open target|Panel slots and layout]] — `here`, `other`, `new` — so a command that opens a panel is written once, for `here`, and takes the target as its first argument;
* what only means something inside a panel is absent — the panel's type, page size, default values and visibility, and `commands.register`.

A command here is meant to ''combine existing commands''. `mf.daemon` is there for
reading what a question needs — the shipped file lists the tag paths for a
completion that way — but something that cannot be said with existing commands is
usually a base command that is missing.

An error thrown by `run` is shown in the status bar, like any command's failure.

!! `mf.invoke`

`mf.invoke([name, ...arguments])` runs a command: the name, then ''one element per
argument''. An element is the argument whole — spaces, quotes and all — so nothing
is quoted or escaped: `mf.invoke(['metarecord-list:insert', 'simplified', text, 'stay'])`
whatever `text` holds. A shell line is one element, as it is typed: `mf.invoke(['!ls -l'])`.

* An argument left out at the end is asked for in the command input, as when the command is typed bare ([[Interactive command arguments]]).
* `mf.ask` in place of an argument asks for that one, ''wherever it stands'': `mf.invoke(['metarecord:bulk', 'selection', 'set', mf.ask, 'int', '4'])` asks which field, and takes the rest as given. `mf.ask({initial: 'rating'})` fills the line first. An optional argument named so is asked too. The last argument, which takes every element left, cannot ask for one of them.
* It resolves once the command has finished — what it opened shown and loaded ([[When a command has finished]]).
* It ''throws'' when the command fails (a `CommandError`, its message the status bar's) or is abandoned at a question with `Escape` (`error.cancelled` is then `true`). A sequence of invocations therefore stops at the first one that did not run; catch it to go on regardless. Left uncaught — `void mf.invoke([...])` behind a button — it stays off the console, since the status bar has said it.

A string is refused, with its array form in the message. Keys, the command input and
[[mf gui command]] keep their text form — an invocation typed or written in a file —
which the GUI splits at the spaces (double quotes group words) before running it
([[Commands]]).

!! Two files

Beside it, `gui/commands.js` has the same shape and holds the commands of the GUI
itself written in JavaScript. Both are loaded, `commands.js` first, and their
commands are one set. They are kept apart for the updates: a shipped change to
`commands.js` is merged into yours ([[Updating the configuration]]), and the merge
cannot collide with commands you added to a file it does not touch. The shipped
`user-commands.js` holds only the example `user:rate`.

What `commands.js` defines is reachable from `user-commands.js` two ways: its
commands through `mf.invoke` like any other, and its helpers — the selected
metarecord (`selectedMetarecord`), its file, the tag paths (`tagPaths`), the tag
kind question (`tagKindArg`)… — by importing them:

```
import { selectedMetarecord } from './commands.js';
```

!! The shipped entries

`commands.js` holds eleven commands of the GUI, written there so that their
questions and what their answers run can be edited:

* [[tag:query]], [[tag:add]] and [[tag:remove]] ([[Tagging from the GUI]]);
* `metarecord:remove` and `metarecord:remove-file` ([[Sending files to the trash]]);
* `file-manager:reveal` and `metarecord-list:folder` ([[Cross-panel selection]]);
* `orphan:delete` and `orphan:detect-delete` ([[Orphans in the GUI]]);
* `repos:switch`, `recent` and `folder:open`, the pickers ([[Frecent folders]]).

They are ordinary entries: removing one removes the command, and a key bound to it
then runs nothing ([[Changing keybindings]]).

!! Names

Any name will do; `user:` keeps yours apart from the shipped ones. A name that is
already a command — of the shell (`quit`, `panel:open`…), of a panel
(`trash:restore`…), or, for an entry of `user-commands.js`, of `commands.js` — is
''left out'': the existing command stays as it is, and the status
bar names the entry that was ignored. An entry can add to the command set, never
replace part of it unnoticed.

!! Applying a change

`config:reload commands` reads both files again: the commands of the previous version
are forgotten, so an entry deleted from the file leaves the command input
([[Reloading the configuration]]).

A file — either one — that cannot be used — a syntax error, an export that is not an object, an
entry without a `run` function — is refused as a whole:

* on a reload, the error goes to the status bar and the commands already there stay;
* at startup, the GUI stops on a banner showing the JavaScript error. No panel is loaded. Correct the file and start again ([[No runtime fallback]]).
